Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 04/10/2023]
1.  DC has been re-calculated from experimental spectroscopic factors presented in Vernotte et al. 1994; Schmidt & Duhm 1970; Green et al. 1970. Zero scattering 
    potential is assumed. Present DC S factor agrees within 10% with experimental total DC value at 1.8 MeV in Wiescher et al. 1980, but the energy dependence 
    differs. Present total DC S factor is a factor of 2 smaller than the result reported in Best et al. 2019 that was obtained from fitting the total resonant + 
    nonresonant yield. It is difficult to see in the latter work how the different fit components give rise to the data. The DC S factor reported in Best et al. 
    2019 disagrees with the upper limit reported in Buckner et al. 2012. Our computed fractional uncertainty for the total DC S factor is <12%, but we will assume 
    a 40% uncertainty throughout.
2.  Er = 206, 261, and 317 keV: wg_pg from weighted average of values listed in Tab. V of Pantaleo et al. 2021.    
3.  Er = 21 keV: partial widths derived from La Cognata et al. 2008, Champagne & Pitt 1986, and priv. comm. by Allen, quoted in Wiescher et al. 1980.
4.  Er = 91 keV: partial widths derived from Best 2019; Bayesian average of Lorenz-Wirzba 1979, La Cognata 2008, Bruno 2019; and priv. comm. by Allen, quoted in 
    Wiescher et al. 1980. 
5.  Er = 145 keV: partial widths derived from (p,g) strength [many measurements], and (p,a) strength and total width reported in Becker 1995.
6.  For Er >= 317 keV: same information as in 2010 MC evaluation, but resonance energies were corrected for nuclear Q values.
